mall suggestions for\nL34@P1: improvement to his work. Making use of these suggestions we prove\nL35@P1: Theorem 1.1. We have\nL36@P1: P(a, q) ≤ Cq5.18\nL37@P1: with an effectively computable constant C.\nL38@P1: In the course of the proof of Theorem 1.1 we improve on several results\nL39@P1: in [8] concerning zero-free regions and zero-density estimates for Dirichlet\nL40@P1: L-functions. Let us cite, for example, the following result.\nL41@P1: Theorem 1.2. There is an effectively computable constant q0, such that\nL42@P1: for q ≥ q0 the function\nL43@P1: Y\nL44@P1: χ (mod q)\nL45@P1: L(s, χ)\nL46@P1: has at most one zero in the region\nL47@P1: σ ≥ 1 −\nL48@P1: 0.440\nL49@P1: log q\nL50@P1: , |t| ≤ 1.\nL51@P1: If this exceptional zero (“Siegel zero”) exists then it is real, simple and be\u0002longs to a non-principal real character.\nL52@P1: Remark. Heath-Brown [8, Theorem 1, p. 268] has proved this estimate\nL53@P1: with c = 0.348 instead of c = 0.440. By a small variation of Heath-Brown’s\nL54@P1: argument, Liu and Wang [14, pp. 345–346] achieved c = 0.364.\nL55@P1: For a motivation as well as a more detailed introduction into this topic\nL56@P1: we refer to [9, §18], [8, pp. 265–270] and [20, pp. 7–10].\nL57@P1: In this paper we refine certain arguments of [8] in the way proposed\nL58@P1: by the nine improvement suggestions in [8, pp. 332–337]. To be precise,\nL59@P1: we use suggestions (2), (5), (7) and (9), the largest contribution to our\nL60@P1: improved estimates being due to suggestion (2). On the other hand, the\nL61@P1: improvement of the admissible value for L resulting from our use of the\nL62@P1: other five suggestions is too small and will not be discussed here.\nL63@P1: Besides some small variations, this paper is a shorter version of our work\nL64@P1: [20] and the reader is referred to that work whenever more details are desired.\nL65@P1: Standard notation from analytic number theory is used. For q ∈ N =\nL66@P1: {1, 2, 3, . . .} we use χ to denote